---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const fractionHref = toolPathBySlug('fraction-calculator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you multiply two matrices? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Leave the operation on Multiply and fill both boxes. One row goes on
      each line. Numbers on a row are separated by spaces or commas. The
      default pair is 1 2 / 3 4 times 5 6 / 7 8. The product is 19 22 on
      the first row and 43 50 on the second.
    </p>
    <p>
      The headline is the top-left entry, 19. Step 1 shows where it came
      from: row 1 of A dotted with column 1 of B, written 1×5 + 2×7 = 19.
      The other entries are listed as rows under that step. You get one
      worked step, then the whole matrix, not a locked expansion.
    </p>
    <p>
      Columns of A must equal rows of B. A 2×3 times a 2×3 fails. The
      message names both shapes. It does not say only that the product is
      undefined. The arithmetic stays in the browser. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Matrix Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="enter">
    <h2>How do you enter a matrix? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Each line is a row. 1 2 3 and then 4 5 6 is a 2×3 matrix. Commas work
      the same way, so 1, 2, 3 is one row of three. A semicolon starts a
      row too. 1, 2; 3, 4 is the 2×2 default written on one line. Blank
      lines are dropped.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every row needs the same count of numbers. If row 2 has two numbers
      and row 1 has three, the page says so and stops. A value that is not
      a number is named by its row. The largest grid is 8 by 8. A wider
      paste is refused before multiply or add runs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Matrix B is required for add, subtract, and multiply. Transpose,
      determinant, and inverse read A only. You can leave B as it is. Those
      three operations ignore it. Clearing B matters only when you switch
      back to an operation that needs two matrices.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Pasting the whole grid onto one line">
      <p>
        A single line is one row. Break rows with a new line or a semicolon,
        or every number lands in row 1 and the shape is wrong.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="shapes">
    <h2>When do the shapes have to match? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Add and subtract need the same shape on both sides. A 2×3 plus a 2×2
      is refused, and the message reads A is 2×3 and B is 2×2. Multiply is
      stricter about a different pair of numbers. The columns of A must
      equal the rows of B. The result then has the rows of A and the
      columns of B.
    </p>
    <p>
      So a 2×3 times a 3×2 is a 2×2. A 2×3 times a 2×3 is not a product.
      The inner 3 and 2 disagree. People meet this when both matrices were
      typed in the same orientation. Transpose one of them and try again.
      The error is the orientation, not a broken formula.
    </p>
    <p>
      The check runs before any dot product. You do not get a partial
      matrix with a hole where the sizes failed. Either the shapes are
      legal and every entry is filled, or you get the sentence that names
      both shapes. That sentence is the whole diagnosis.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Name the shapes">
      <p>
        A is 2×3 and B is 2×3. Multiply needs the inner sizes to match.
        Once you can see both shapes, the fix is usually to flip one matrix.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="steps">
    <h2>What do the first steps show? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The first step is one entry, written out. For a product it is the
      first dot product. For a sum it is the first pair added, such as
      1 + 5 = 6 if you switch the default matrices to Add. For a difference
      it is the same pair subtracted. You can check that line by hand, then
      trust the remaining rows.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 2 by 2 determinant shows the textbook line. 1×4 - 2×3 = -2 for the
      default matrix. Larger square matrices report the value from
      elimination instead of a wall of minors. An inverse reports the
      top-left entry after Gauss-Jordan on the augmented matrix. The row
      list is the rest of the inverse.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not a full tableau and not reduced row echelon form. There
      is no RREF operation. If a course asks you to show every row operation,
      write those lines yourself from the one step and the finished matrix.
      The page will not pretend a single dot product is the entire proof.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="One step, then the matrix">
      <p>
        The step is the check you can do in your head. The rows under it
        are the rest of the answer. Both stay on the page.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="det">
    <h2>How do determinant and inverse differ? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The determinant is one number. The inverse is another matrix. Both
      need a square A. A 2×3 has neither. The message names that shape and
      stops. For a 2×2, determinant is a×d - b×c. The default gives -2,
      which is not zero, so the inverse exists.
    </p>
    <p>
      The inverse of 1 2 / 3 4 has top-left entry -2, then 1 on the rest
      of the first row, and 1.5 and -0.5 on the second. Multiply that
      inverse by the original and you should get the identity, 1 0 / 0 1.
      Switch back to Multiply and paste the inverse into B if you want to
      see that product.
    </p>
    <p>
      Determinant 0 means no inverse. 1 2 / 2 4 is the example: the second
      row is twice the first, and the page says the determinant is 0, so
      this matrix has no inverse. It does not print a matrix of zeros or
      of infinities. Zero determinant is a stop, not a result grid.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Inverting a singular matrix">
      <p>
        If the rows are multiples of each other, stop. There is no inverse
        to copy into the next line of a solution.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="rounding">
    <h2>Why does 0.1 plus 0.2 print as 0.3? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Binary floating point cannot store 0.1 exactly. Adding 0.1 and 0.2
      produces a value that prints as 0.30000000000000004 if you show every
      bit. A matrix of tenths would then look unequal to the pencil answer
      even when the arithmetic is right. This page tidies each entry.
    </p>
    <p>
      A value that is within a hair of a whole number prints as that whole
      number. A tenth prints as 0.3, not as a long tail. The assumption
      under the result says this in one line. The tidy step does not change
      a real difference, such as 19 versus 19.5. It only removes noise.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fractions you want to keep exact, such as 1/3, should be entered as
      a decimal you accept, or reduced on the <a href={fractionHref}>fraction
      calculator</a> first. This page does not do fraction arithmetic. It
      multiplies the decimals you typed, then tidies the printout.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="choose">
    <h2>Which operation should you pick? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Multiply when a textbook says product or composition. Add when it
      says sum. Subtract when it says difference. Transpose when it says
      flip the rows into columns. Determinant when it asks for one number
      from a square matrix. Inverse when it asks for the matrix that
      multiplies back to the identity.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read the shape note on the result. A 2×2 result from a 2×3 times a
      3×2 is the legal size. If you expected a 2×3, the operation or the
      orientation is wrong, even when no error appeared. The rows under
      the headline are the check. Count them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then change one entry and look again. The step and the rows update
      together. That loop is the reason to use the page instead of a single
      chat reply: the grid stays put, and the shape sentence appears only
      when the sizes actually disagree. Runs entirely on your device.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
